  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1040 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nMonday through Saturday:\n\nA high-amplitude trough in the 500-300 mb jet over the Rocky\nMountains Sunday night will slowly track eastward, with\nincreasingly diffluent flow on it's east edge. Strong surface low\npressure develops beneath this (NE Colorado) and tracks\nnortheastward toward eastern South Dakota by Monday evening.\nWorking with the departed high pressure system over the Mid\nAtlantic CONUS, this system will drive breezy south winds and\nstrong WAA across WI on Monday. WAA-driven rain showers are \nexpected to build eastward into southern WI throughout Monday\n(35-90% chances), overspreading the region and continuing Monday \nnight through Tuesday in the warm sector of this system (75-100% \nchances). 18z GEFS and ECMWF-ENS guidance continues to hint at \nsome weak MUCAPE entering the region through this period, so we \ncan't rule out a few thunderstorms mixing in with the widespread \nrainfall. Very few ensemble members allow sufficient MUCAPE for \nthunder during the day Monday, but we introduce slight thunder \nchances to the forecast Monday night into Tuesday as ensemble \nmembership for MUCAPE increases. Given that even the more \naggressive ensemble members are in the ballpark of 1,000 joules,\nwe project instability to be too weak to support strong \nthunderstorms. That said, the presence of any convective \nupdrafts could lead to some brief locally-heavy downpours. \nEnsemble Mean PWAT is around 1.3\" Monday, increasing to around \n1.6\" Monday night into Tuesday coincident with a healthy LLJ \noverhead. Not expecting any widespread flooding threat at this \ntime given the antecedent dry weather. NBM QPF totals for the \nsynoptic rainfall are around 0.3-0.7 inches with this system \nMonday-Tuesday, and any higher totals would likely be localized \nto the vicinity of any healthier updrafts / thunderstorms. \n\nThe warmth with this system is expected to peak on Tuesday, with\nhighs around 70 degrees in the forecast. The MKE and MSN daily\nhigh temp records are 74 and 76 degrees (respectively) for Nov 3,\nso not quite 'record breaking' yet, but certainly well above \nnormal for this time of year. \n\nFrom Tuesday night onwards, model solutions begin to diverge, and\nthe forecast becomes less certain. Some model solutions rapidly\nweaken the low or eject it northeastward, with the weakened /\ndeparted low forcing a cold front across southern WI. Other\nsolutions stall the low in or around WI. Both of these scenarios \nwould continue the aforementioned shower / storm chances midweek \n(50-70% rain chances), and both could lead to continued rain \nchances for the late half of the work week either via lingering \nfrontal baroclinicity or a lingering cutoff-low respectively \n(20-45% rain chances). \n\nThe general model consensus is relatively dry and quiet conditions\nnext weekend in the wake of this system, though predictability is\nlow. \n\nSheppard",
